 # TangoGS
 
-Also called: Gauss What You Need
+Also called: Capture-Conditioned Learning Allowance, Quality-Feedback Pruning
 
 ## Overview
-TangoGS is a density control strategy for 3D Gaussian Splatting that automatically scales the number of Gaussian primitives to match the complexity of the input capture without per-scene tuning [S1, unofficial]. It combines a capture-derived "learning allowance" (model sizing based on track-adjusted pixel count) with training-based quality feedback (PSNR-driven pruning) to determine the final Gaussian population [S1, unofficial]. Unlike sibling methods that rely on fixed thresholds or per-capture calibration, TangoGS uses a shared configuration across diverse scene scales, achieving comparable or better PSNR with significantly fewer Gaussians than baselines like LeGS or 3DGS [S1, unofficial].
+TangoGS is a density control strategy for 3D Gaussian Splatting that automatically scales the Gaussian population across different capture sizes without per-capture retuning [S1, unofficial]. It combines a capture-derived learning allowance (model sizing) with training-based adaptation (quality feedback) to determine the final number of Gaussians [S1, unofficial]. Unlike sibling methods that rely on fixed defaults or total pixel counts, TangoGS ensures the model budget is invariant to redundant views by using track-adjusted pixels, while using reconstruction quality to dynamically prune or retain capacity [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-TangoGS operates on the set of 3D Gaussians $\mathcal{G}$ defined by the parent [Parent]. The method manages the population size through three phases: allowance calculation, budgeted densification with soft pruning, and final selection [S1, unofficial].
+TangoGS operates in three phases: allowance calculation, adaptive densification/pruning, and final selection. It uses the LiteGS implementation for scoring and optimization [S1, unofficial].
 
